{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.AbsoluteValue.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.proton_count_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.proton_count_unique","kind":"theorem","line":182,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ElectronCountRigidity.lean#L182-L198","doc":"**The proton instance.**  At `d = dim V_{(2,1)} = 2` the rigidity window is\nexactly one electron mass.\n\nRead against the numerics (`me_additive_term_structure.py`): the observed\nresidual at `c = |S₃| = 6` is `≈ 1.3·10⁻⁶ MeV`, far inside the window, while\nthe nearest competing integer `c = 7` misses by `≈ 1.02 MeV` — twice outside\nit.  So `|S₃|` is the unique integer count compatible with the measured proton\nmass. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.count_unique_of_close","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.count_unique_of_close","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ElectronCountRigidity.lean#L135-L178","doc":"**Count rigidity.**  If integer counts `c` and `c'` both reproduce the\nobserved mass `m_obs` to within `d·mₑ/2`, then `c = c'`.\n\nFor the corpus instance `d = 2` the tolerance is exactly one electron mass:\n*any* measurement of `m_p` good to better than `mₑ ≈ 0.511 MeV` pins the\nelectron count uniquely.  The actual residual at `c = 6` is `≈ 1.3·10⁻⁶ MeV`,\nroughly `4·10⁵` times inside the window — so the count is not merely\ndetermined, it is determined with enormous margin.\n\nThis is the formal statement that the additive term is **not** a unit\nconvention: a convention admits many equally good values, and this admits\nexactly one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_delete_term","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_delete_term","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ElectronCountRigidity.lean#L122-L126","doc":"Deleting the additive term entirely (count `0`) shifts the mass by exactly\n`d · c · mₑ` — for the corpus instance, `2 · 6 · mₑ = 12 mₑ ≈ 6.13 MeV`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_sub_count","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_sub_count","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ElectronCountRigidity.lean#L110-L120","doc":"**Exact gap between counts.**  Changing the electron count from `c` to `c'`\nshifts the mass by exactly `d · mₑ · (c' - c)`, independently of the\nconstituent energy `E`.\n\nThe `E`-independence matters: the shift cannot be reabsorbed by re-tuning the\nmultiplicative dressing at fixed `c`, because the dressing enters through `E`\nand cancels here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_homogeneous","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_homogeneous","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ElectronCountRigidity.lean#L94-L103","doc":"**Relational mass — homogeneity of degree 1 in `mₑ`.**  Rescaling the\nelectron mass rescales the nucleon mass by exactly the same factor.\n\nThis is the falsifiable content of \"mass is relational\": a formula carrying an\n`mₑ²` term, or an additive constant with mass dimension not supplied by `mₑ`,\nwould fail this. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_factors","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass_factors","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ElectronCountRigidity.lean#L85-L92","doc":"**Relational mass — the factorisation.**  When the constituent energy is\nitself `mₑ` times a dimensionless factor `G` (as it is: `E = m_μ·F_Bor` with\n`m_μ = mₑ·Vol(4₁)/ℏ_q²`), the whole mass is `mₑ` times a dimensionless number.\n\nThere is no intrinsic mass in the formula: only the ratio `m/mₑ` is predicted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ElectronCountRigidity.nucleonMass","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/ElectronCountRigidity.lean#L73-L81","doc":"The canonical nucleon-mass shape: a block multiplicity `d`, a constituent\nenergy `E`, and an integer count `c` of subtracted electron masses `mₑ`.\n\n    `nucleonMass d E c mₑ = d * (E - c * mₑ)`\n\nInstantiated in the corpus at `d = dim V_{(2,1)} = 2`, `c = |S₃| = 6`,\n`E = m_μ · F_Bor`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}